"""fetch-fonts: vendor Interleaf's text fonts into interleaf/fonts/ (spec 002).

    fetch-fonts              download, check, cut down where allowed, write interleaf/fonts/
    fetch-fonts --list       print the faces it writes

The families are the ones the component styles use (interleaf/components/bundle.css):
IBM Plex Sans and Mono (White), Source Serif 4 (Editorial), EB Garamond (Stationery) and
Kalam (White's hand). All are under the SIL Open Font License 1.1; each family's licence is
written beside its fonts.

IBM Plex is copied byte for byte. Its licence reserves the font name "Plex", and condition 3
of the OFL forbids that name on a modified version, which cutting or converting a font is.
PLEX therefore takes IBM's own web builds from the npm releases, each checked by sha256 (the
tarball and every file), with the licence from the same release. Never run them through cut().

The other three families have no reserved font name. FAMILIES takes them from a pinned
google/fonts commit (sha256) and keeps only what the styles use: the weights (a variable
font is cut down to the weight range) and the Latin letters (with the extensions),
punctuation, arrows and symbols (UNICODES); all OpenType features stay (small caps, old-style
figures). A family whose licence reserves a name is refused there.

The output is woff2, plus interleaf/fonts/fonts.css with one @font-face per file. Then run
tools/gen-interleaf (make generate): it copies the fonts into web/.

Needs python3 with fontTools and brotli (Alpine: py3-fonttools py3-brotli; tools/in-alpine
has both) and network access. The output is the same for the same sources and fontTools.
"""
import hashlib
import io
import os
import shutil
import sys
import tarfile
import urllib.request

ROOT = os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)))
OUT = os.path.join(ROOT, "interleaf", "fonts")
GF = "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/google/fonts/0b58fb370093f9a9f4ff785d94405710b79de67c/ofl/"

